 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Depth Alignment:** The accuracy of the composition depends entirely on the correct mapping between screen depth and 3D texture coordinates. Misalignment causes the volume to appear detached from the geometry [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Opaque Scene Requirement:** This method requires a pre-existing opaque scene depth buffer. It cannot render transparent surfaces or complex depth ordering between volume and geometry without additional passes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Opaque Scene Requirement:** This method requires a pre-existing opaque scene depth buffer for coordinate mapping [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Coordinate Precision:** The accuracy of the composition depends on the precision of the depth-to-texture coordinate conversion [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Volumetric Lighting Composition
-A variant that composites direct illumination, volumetric shadows, and caustic buffers. It renders surface caustics to a separate render target to allow for low-pass filtering before composition [S2].
+A variant that composites direct illumination, volumetric shadows, and caustic buffers [S2]. It renders surface caustics to a separate render target to allow for low-pass filtering before composition [S2].
 
 ### Scene Editing
-A variant used in editable Gaussian splatting models (iVR-GS). It allows real-time editing of the composed volume by replacing palette colors, scaling opacity, and adjusting shading attributes ($k_a, k_d, k_s, \beta$) to change light magnitude and direction using the Blinn-Phong equation [S3].
+A variant used in editable Gaussian splatting models (iVR-GS) [S3]. It allows real-time editing by replacing palette color $c_p$ with a new palette color $c'_p$, adjusting opacity by directly scaling the opacity term, and scaling attributes $k_a, k_d, k_s, \beta$ to adjust shading magnitudes [S3]. It renders with a new light direction using the Blinn-Phong equation based on primitive normals $n$ [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per pixel for the composition step, as it involves a single texture lookup and arithmetic operation. The complexity lies in the pre-computation of the 3D texture.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the resolution of the 3D texture. Requires storage for the transmittance and in-scattered light volumes.
-*   **Strengths:** High performance due to hardware interpolation and decoupling of volume and surface rendering. Simple implementation in post-processing.
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to volumes that can be pre-computed into a 3D texture. Struggles with dynamic geometry or complex depth interactions between volume and transparent surfaces. Accuracy depends on the resolution of the 3D texture and the precision of the depth-to-texture coordinate conversion.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per pixel for the composition step, as it involves a single texture lookup and arithmetic operation [G]. The overall complexity is dominated by the pre-computation of the 3D texture [G].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the resolution of the 3D texture, requiring storage for transmittance and in-scattered light volumes [G].
+*   **Strengths:** High performance due to hardware interpolation and decoupling of volume and surface rendering [G].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited to volumes that can be pre-computed into a 3D texture [G].
 
 ## Background
-This method is described in the context of implementing volumetric fog in OpenGL using 3D textures. It leverages hardware capabilities for 3D texture sampling to achieve real-time performance for participating media [S1, unofficial].
